<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Lab 1, Due: Monday, September 21</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be completed
      in groups of 2 or 3 students.  You only need to hand in one copy
      per group, but please include everyone's name.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Names:</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>Note:</alert> There are two aims for this lab.  First, I
      want to introduce you to spreadsheets as a tool for
      understanding some ideas in calculus.  At the beginning of the
      class, I will demonstrate for you some fundamental things: how
      to enter data in cells, how to refer to other cells and how to
      copy information from one cell to another.  Second, I would like
      to review how definite integrals are constructed; namely, I
      would like for you to think of an integral as a fancy sum.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Remember that if we have a function <m>f(x)</m>, we can
        define the definite integral
        <md>
          \int_a^b f(x)~dx
        </md>
        by looking at either left sums <m>L_n</m> or right sums
        <m>R_n</m>.  To form these, we choose some value for
        <m>n</m> and define <m>\Delta x = \frac{b-a}{n}</m> and
        the points <m>x_i=a+i\Delta x</m>.  The left and right
        Riemann sums are
        <md>
          <mrow>
            L_n\amp=\amp f(x_0)\Delta x + f(x_1)\Delta x + \ldots
            + f(x_{n-1})\Delta x = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1}f(x_i)\Delta x
          </mrow> 
          <mrow>
            R_n\amp=\amp f(x_1)\Delta x + f(x_2)\Delta x + \ldots
            + f(x_{n})\Delta x = \sum_{i=1}^nf(x_i)\Delta x
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The definite integral is then defined as
        <md>
          \int_a^b f(x)~dx  =\lim_{n\to\infty}L_n = \lim_{n\to\infty}R_n.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let's now consider the function <m>f(x) = x^3</m> and the
          definite integral
          <md>
            \int_0^1 x^3~dx
          </md>.
          First off, use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate
          this integral exactly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Set up a Google sheet as shown below to evaluate the Riemann
          sums with <m>n = 10</m>.  The first column is <m>\Delta x</m>.
          The second column starts at <m>0</m> and each subsequent entry
          is obtained by adding <m>\Delta x</m> to the entry above.
          This generates the points <m>x_i</m>.  The third column is
          simply the function <m>f(x)=x^3</m> evaluated at <m>x_i</m>.
          The fourth column will add the terms for the left sum, and the
          fifth column will add the terms for the right sum.  To obtain
          the entry in one row, simply add the appropriate term
          <m>f(x_i)\Delta x</m> to the entry directly above.
        </p>
        <p>
          A convenient way to set this up is to enter 0.1 for
          <m>\Delta x</m> in cell <term>A2</term>, enter "=A2" in cell
          <term>A3</term> and copy that entry into the rest of the
          column.  That way, if you change <m>\Delta x</m> in one
          place, it will change everywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          <tabular>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>\Delta x</m></cell>
              <cell><m>x_i</m></cell>
              <cell><m>f(x_i)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>L_n</m></cell>
              <cell><m>R_n</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1000</cell>
              <cell>0.0000</cell>
              <cell>0.0000</cell>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1000</cell>
              <cell>0.1000</cell>
              <cell>0.0010</cell>
              <cell>0.0000</cell>
              <cell>0.0001</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1000</cell>
              <cell>0.2000</cell>
              <cell>0.0080</cell>
              <cell>0.0001</cell>
              <cell>0.0009</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1000</cell>
              <cell>0.3000</cell>
              <cell>0.0270</cell>
              <cell>0.0009</cell>
              <cell>0.0036</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\vdots</m></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </p>
        <p>
          What do you find for the Riemann sums, <m>L_{10}</m> and
          <m>R_{10}</m>?  Be sure that you have actually included exactly
          10 terms in your approximations.  State your results with 6
          digits after the decimal place.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L_{10} \amp = \underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
            <intertext>
            </intertext>
            <mrow>
              R_{10} \amp =\underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now repeat this using <m>n = 100</m>.  This corresponds to
          <m>\Delta x = 0.01</m>.  If you set up your earlier worksheet
          well, you should only need to change the entry for
          <m>\Delta x</m> in one location.  What do you find for the
          Riemann sums?  State your result with 6 digits after the
          decimal place.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L_{100} \amp = \underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
            <intertext>
            </intertext>
            <mrow>
              R_{100} \amp =\underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Repeat using <m>n = 1000</m>.  What do you find for the
          Riemann sums?  State your result with 6 digits after the
          decimal place.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L_{1000} \amp = \underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
            <intertext>
            </intertext>
            <mrow>
              R_{1000} \amp =\underline{\hspace{2in}}
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Shown below is the graph of the function <m>f(x)=x^3</m>.
          Sketch in the rectangles you used in building the left Riemann
          sum with <m>\Delta x = 0.1</m>.  Then use your figure to
          explain why the approximations <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m> to
          the definite integral you found above are either too large or
          too small.
        </p>
        <sidebyside width="45%" margins="0% 55%">
          <image>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="08-lab1-cubic">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/08-lab-1-cubic.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now fill in the following table:</p>
        <p>
          <tabular>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>\Delta x</m></cell>
              <cell><m>L_n</m></cell>
              <cell>Error</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.1 <m>\vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\hphantom{hello there hello}</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\hphantom{hello there hello}</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.01 <m>\vphantom{\Huge p}</m></cell>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>0.001 <m>\vphantom{\Huge p}</m></cell>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </p>
        <p>
          What do you notice about the error?  Try to be quantitative
          rather than just qualitative; that is, when the width of the
          intervals <m>\Delta x</m> decreases by a factor of one tenth,
          by roughly what factor does the error decrease?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The definite integral
        <md>\int_0^1e^{-x^2}~dx</md>
        cannot be evaluated using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus
        because there is not an antiderivative that can be found in
        terms of familiar functions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using left and right Riemann sums, give an estimate of this
        definite integral with an error of no more than <m>0.005</m>.
        Explain how you've done this with left and right Riemann sums.
        Do not evaluate the integral on your calculator to estimate
        the error.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note: to evaluate an exponential in a spreadsheet, use
        "= EXP( ... )".
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
